#: ../../source/open_readme.md:1
msgid "open"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:3
msgid "Open stuff like URLs, files, executables. Cross-platform."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:5
msgid ""
"This is meant to be used in command-line tools and scripts, not in the "
"browser."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:7
msgid ""
"If you need this for Electron, use "
"[`shell.openPath()`](https://www.electronjs.org/docs/api/shell#shellopenpathpath)"
" instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:9
msgid ""
"Note: The original [`open` package](https://github.com/pwnall/node-open) "
"was previously deprecated in favor of this package, and we got the name, "
"so this package is now named `open` instead of `opn`. If you're upgrading"
" from the original `open` package (`open@0.0.5` or lower), keep in mind "
"that the API is different."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:11
msgid "Why?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:13
msgid "Actively maintained."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:14
msgid "Supports app arguments."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:15
msgid "Safer as it uses `spawn` instead of `exec`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:16
msgid "Fixes most of the open original `node-open` issues."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:17
msgid ""
"Includes the latest [`xdg-open` script](https://cgit.freedesktop.org/xdg"
"/xdg-utils/commit/?id=c55122295c2a480fa721a9614f0e2d42b2949c18) for "
"Linux."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:18
msgid "Supports WSL paths to Windows apps."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:20
msgid "Install"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:26
msgid "Usage"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:47
msgid "API"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:49
msgid ""
"It uses the command `open` on macOS, `start` on Windows and `xdg-open` on"
" other platforms."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:51
msgid "open(target, options?)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:53
msgid ""
"Returns a promise for the [spawned child "
"process](https://nodejs.org/api/child_process.html#child_process_class_childprocess)."
" You would normally not need to use this for anything, but it can be "
"useful if you'd like to attach custom event listeners or perform other "
"operations directly on the spawned process."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:55
msgid "target"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:57
msgid "Type: `string`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:59
msgid "The thing you want to open. Can be a URL, file, or executable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:61
msgid ""
"Opens in the default app for the file type. For example, URLs opens in "
"your default browser."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:63
msgid "options"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:65
msgid "Type: `object`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:67
msgid "wait"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:69 ../../source/open_readme.md:80
#: ../../source/open_readme.md:97 ../../source/open_readme.md:107
msgid "Type: `boolean` Default: `false`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:72
msgid ""
"Wait for the opened app to exit before fulfilling the promise. If `false`"
" it's fulfilled immediately when opening the app."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:74
msgid "Note that it waits for the app to exit, not just for the window to close."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:76
msgid ""
"On Windows, you have to explicitly specify an app for it to be able to "
"wait."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:78
msgid "background <sup>(macOS only)</sup>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:83
msgid "Do not bring the app to the foreground."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:85
msgid "app"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:87
msgid "Type: `string | string[]`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:89
msgid ""
"Specify the app to open the `target` with, or an array with the app and "
"app arguments."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:91
msgid ""
"The app name is platform dependent. Don't hard code it in reusable "
"modules. For example, Chrome is `google chrome` on macOS, `google-chrome`"
" on Linux and `chrome` on Windows."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:93
msgid ""
"You may also pass in the app's full path. For example on WSL, this can be"
" `/mnt/c/Program Files (x86)/Google/Chrome/Application/chrome.exe` for "
"the Windows installation of Chrome."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:95
msgid "url"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:100
msgid ""
"Uses `URL` to encode the target before executing it.<br> We do not "
"recommend using it on targets that are not URLs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:103
msgid ""
"Especially useful when dealing with the [double-quotes on Windows"
"](#double-quotes-on-windows) caveat."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:105
msgid "allowNonzeroExitCode"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:110
msgid ""
"Allow the opened app to exit with nonzero exit code when the `wait` "
"option is `true`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:112
msgid ""
"We do not recommend setting this option. The convention for success is "
"exit code zero."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:114
msgid "Caveats"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:116
msgid "Double-quotes on Windows"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:118
msgid ""
"TL;DR: All double-quotes are stripped from the `target` and do not get to"
" your desired destination (on Windows!)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:120
msgid ""
"Due to specific behaviors of Window's Command Prompt (`cmd.exe`) "
"regarding ampersand (`&`) characters breaking commands and URLs, double-"
"quotes are now a special case."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:122
msgid ""
"The solution ([#146](https://github.com/sindresorhus/open/pull/146)) to "
"this and other problems was to leverage the fact that `cmd.exe` "
"interprets a double-quoted argument as a plain text argument just by "
"quoting it (like Node.js already does). Unfortunately, `cmd.exe` can only"
" do **one** of two things: handle them all **OR** not handle them at all."
" As per its own documentation:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:124
msgid ""
"*If /C or /K is specified, then the remainder of the command line after "
"the switch is processed as a command line, where the following logic is "
"used to process quote (\") characters:*"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:126
msgid ""
"*If all of the following conditions are met, then quote characters on the"
" command line are preserved:*"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:127
msgid "*no /S switch*"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:128
msgid "*exactly two quote characters*"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:129
msgid ""
"*no special characters between the two quote characters, where special is"
" one of: &<>()@^|*"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:130
msgid ""
"*there are one or more whitespace characters between the two quote "
"characters*"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:131
msgid ""
"*the string between the two quote characters is the name of an executable"
" file.*"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:133
msgid ""
"*Otherwise, old behavior is to see if the first character is a quote "
"character and if so, strip the leading character and remove the last "
"quote character on the command line, preserving any text after the last "
"quote character.*"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:135
msgid ""
"The option that solved all of the problems was the second one, and for "
"additional behavior consistency we're also now using the `/S` switch, so "
"we **always** get the second option. The caveat is that this built-in "
"double-quotes handling ends up stripping all of them from the command "
"line and so far we weren't able to find an escaping method that works (if"
" you do, please feel free to contribute!)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/open_readme.md:137
msgid ""
"To make this caveat somewhat less impactful (at least for URLs), check "
"out the [url option](#url). Double-quotes will be \"preserved\" when "
"using it with an URL."
msgstr ""

